# Line endings are pinned to LF for everything the toolchain reads.
#
# This is not style. On Windows, git converts to CRLF on checkout by default, so
# a run's isolated worktree gets CRLF sources while the developer's tree has LF —
# and `gofmt -l` reports every converted file as needing a rewrite. The gate then
# refuses every run with a red format check over a tree nobody edited, and the
# fleet stalls with a message that describes the wrong problem entirely.
*.go      text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf

# There are deliberately no rules here for served assets — HTML, CSS, images.
# The rules that were here covered a site tree that has been scrapped, and a
# pinned line ending for a file type the repository does not hold is a rule
# nobody can test: the first file to arrive under it would be the one that finds
# out whether it was still right. A project that adds such a tree adds its rules
# with it, next to the files they govern.
